---
title: Understanding the Nonfarm Payroll Data
description: Basic Information, Release Schedule, Revisions, Seasoonal Adjustment, and more
date: 2026-09-14
categories: [labor market, nonfarm payroll]
---

Nonfarm payrolls is one of the most important, if not the most important, labor market indicators. There is a carefully designed statistical methodology, making the measure rather hard to interpret beyond the headline. This post attempts to provide an overview, as well as to shed light on some technical details about the indicator, especially the revisions and seasonal adjustment.

# I. What the number is

- **Definition:** The nonfarm payroll collects data on payrolls covering 80% of paid workers who contribute to the U.S. GDP.[^1]
- **Source & Frequency:** The Bureau of Labor Statistics (BLS) collects payroll data from private and government entities [on a monthly basis]{.underline} and usually publishes the data on the [first Friday of each month]{.underline} at 8:30 a.m. ET.
- **Reference Period:** Employment is counted as people on the establishment payrolls who work or receive payments for any part of the pay period that includes the [12th day of the month]{.underline}.[^2]
- **The Measure Excludes:** farm employees, active-duty military members, unpaid volunteers for non-profit organizations, sole proprietors, and private household employees.[^3]

[^1]: <https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/payems>

[^2]: BLS, *CES Frequently Asked Questions*. <https://www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cesfaq.htm>

[^3]: <https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/payems>

## Why are certain groups excluded?

- **Agriculture**
  - CES draws its sample based on administrative records from the Office of Unemployment Insurance (UI). This means workers who are not covered under the government's unemployment insurance program wouldn't be counted. In agriculture, however, workers tend to have exemptions to requirements for unemployment coverage.
  - In addition, businesses are frequently family-owned (i.e. sole proprietors), which make them out of scope for CES.[^4]
  - Lastly, the sample size for farming would be too small to make statistically sound estimates (0.9%–1.2% of total employment).[^5]
  - This doesn't mean that farming employment is completely unaccounted for. The U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) conducts surveys twice every year to collect data on agricultural workers.
- **Military:** The nonfarm payroll report primarily covers employment that is associated with the business cycle. Military employment changes primarily based on the need for national defense, not the performance of the economy.

[^4]: BLS, *Why doesn't CES collect agricultural employment?* <https://www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cesfaq.htm>

[^5]: USDA ERS. <https://www.ers.usda.gov/data-products/chart-gallery/58282>

# II. How the number is obtained

## Sources of data

::: {.content-hidden unless-meta="show-notes"}
> "Each month the CES program surveys about 119,000 businesses and government agencies, representing approximately 622,000 individual worksites... The active sample includes approximately 26 percent of all nonfarm payroll jobs."

> "About 97 percent of total nonfarm employment within the scope of the establishment survey is covered by UI and is available to the CES program via QCEW records."

> "In 2025, BLS derived totals of 12.1 million establishments, 155.7 million employed, and \$12.3 trillion in wages, from reports submitted to state workforce agencies by every employer covered by UI or by UCFE."

QCEW is published "within 6 months after the end of each referenced quarter" and covers "more than 95 percent of U.S. jobs."

\[NOTE TO SELF — 95% and 97% are different denominators, not a contradiction. 95% is of *all US jobs* (QCEW's own framing); 97% is of *nonfarm employment within the scope of the establishment survey* (the CES framing). Don't let them collide in one sentence.\]

\[WRITE — the core trade-off: CES is fast and partial, QCEW is complete and slow. Everything in Parts III and IV follows from this one design choice.\]
:::

The Quarterly Census of Employment and Wages (QCEW) program collects data based on state-level Unemployment Insurance (UI) administrative records. It is able to cover as much as 97% of total nonfarm employment. However, the cost of completeness is less frequent update, and the data are only published on a quarterly basis. To improve timeliness and better gauge labor market condition, the BLS utilizes the Current Employment Statistics (CES), which draws a representative sample from the QCEW. Below is a comparison table of the two surveys.

```{r}
#| label: tbl-ces-qcew
#| tbl-cap: "Comparison Table: CES vs. QCEW (data as of 2025)"
#| echo: false

library(knitr)
data.frame(
  x = c("Source", "Units covered", "Employment covered", "Frequency", "Lag"),
  ces = c("Sample survey, UI-derived frame", "~119,000 firms / ~622,000 worksites[^6]",
          "~26% of nonfarm payroll jobs", "Monthly", "~3 weeks"),
  qcew = c("Near-census of UI tax records", "~12.1 million establishments[^8]",
           "~97% of in-scope nonfarm employment[^7] / ~95% of all U.S. jobs[^9]", "Quarterly", "within 6 months")
) |> kable(col.names = c("", "CES", "QCEW"), align = "c")
```

The defect of the CES sample is that it cannot collect timely data on business births, nor can it access accurate data on business deaths. This is why the BLS develops the birth-death model to estimate the impact on employment from births and deaths of firms.

## The birth-death model

- **Imputation.** BLS "excludes employment losses due to business deaths from sample-based estimation in order to offset the missing employment gains from business births." In practice, "not reflecting sample units going out of business and imputing to them the same trend as the other firms in the sample."[^6] BLS says this step "accounts for most of the birth and death employment."

- **An ARIMA model on the residual only**, estimating "the residual birth-death employment not accounted for by the imputation," fitted on the trailing five years.[^7]

[^6]: BLS, *CES Net Birth-Death Model*. <https://www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cesbd.htm>

[^7]: BLS, *CES Net Birth-Death Model*. <https://www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cesbd.htm>

::: {.content-hidden unless-meta="show-notes"}
Net birth-death, total nonfarm, as forecast:

- **+372,000** across April–December 2025 (nine months)

- **+861,000** across January–August 2026 (eight months).[^8]

- **−911,000** after benchmark revision

the mechanism of failure. The model does not "invent" jobs; it assumes deaths are a usable proxy for unobserved births. "If employment losses from firm deaths begin exceeding employment gains from new firms, using deaths as a proxy for unobserved births will result in an overestimation of job creation."[^9]
:::

[^8]: BLS, *CES Net Birth-Death Model*. <https://www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cesbd.htm>

[^9]: Michael W. Horrigan, *BLS revisions to payroll data are concerning, but not for the reasons the Trump administration thinks*, W.E. Upjohn Institute. <https://www.upjohn.org/horrigan-bls-revisions-payroll-data-are-concerning-not-reasons-trump-administration-thinks>

### A Falling Response Rate

| Year | Overall response rate | Collected at 1st release | Collected at final release |
|:----------------:|:----------------:|:----------------:|:-----------------:|
| 2016 | 60.8 | 74.7 | 95.7 |
| 2019 | 59.3 | 73.9 | 93.2 |
| 2022 | 45.2 | 65.4 | 94.0 |
| 2025 | 42.2 | 65.2 | 91.5 |
| 2026 | 43.3 | 63.0 | — |

: CES response and collection rates, annual averages, percent. Source: BLS Office of Survey Methods Research.

The share of *units* answering has fallen roughly 18 points since 2016. The share of *employment* collected by final closing has barely moved. Both are true because collection is employment-weighted: the large employers still report. What the survey is losing is small establishments, which is the same population the birth-death model is extrapolating over.

## Seasonal adjustment

Seasonal adjustments make it easier to determine whether an employment change is due to normal changes throughout the year or an indicator of anomalies. For example, This is a regular seasonal fluctuation, which is reflected in the NSA data, but more hiring or less hiring in this case does not suggest expansion nor recession.

- The question seasonal adjustment answers is "what does a normal January look like?" X-11, the engine inside X-13, answers it by **averaging, not by fitting a model**. Take the same calendar month across several years and average it. Divide it out of the raw series and what is left is trend plus noise. Smooth that to get a cleaner trend, take the trend back out, re-estimate the monthly pattern on the cleaner residual, and go round again. After three or four passes the estimate stops moving.
- Two things follow directly from *averaging* rather than *fitting*. **First**, every new observation changes the average, so *last* January's factor moves when *this* January arrives. **Second**, the newest months sit at the end of the sample with no future to average over, so X-13 forecasts the series forward and averages over the forecast. Those are the least stable factors in the series.
- CES runs this **concurrently**: factors are recomputed every month with the latest data rather than fixed in advance, and the models are re-specified annually.[^10] X-13 wraps X-11 in a regARIMA step that first strips out outliers, trading-day effects and moving holidays (CES adjusts for Easter and Labor Day) so the averaging is not contaminated by them.
- **The seasonally adjusted history changes every month even when not one additional survey response arrives**

[^10]: BLS, *CES Seasonal Adjustment*. <https://www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cesseasadj.htm>

| January | Unadjusted | Adjusted | Adjustment |
|:-------:|:----------:|:--------:|:----------:|
|  2023   |   −2,505   |   +517   |   +3,022   |
|  2024   |   −2,635   |   +353   |   +2,988   |
|  2025   |   −2,852   |   +143   |   +2,995   |
|  2026   |   −2,649   |   +130   |   +2,779   |

: NSA vs. SA for January (2023–2026). Source: author's calculations from ALFRED vintages of `PAYEMS` and `PAYNSA`.

\[The point: the headline is a residual. Roughly three million jobs genuinely come off payrolls every January; the adjustment adds back very slightly less; the difference is what gets reported and argued about. A 5% error in an adjustment that size would be 150,000 — larger than most of the monthly prints in this table.\]

# III. How the number changes

Three structurally different operations all get called "a revision," and they have different causes, sizes and meanings.

```{r}
#| label: tbl-vocab
#| tbl-cap: "Vocabulary for CES payroll estimates and their revisions."
#| echo: false

library(knitr)
data.frame(
  Colloquial = c("initial print", "first revision", "second revision",
                 "the big revision", "annual benchmark"),
  BLS = c("first preliminary estimate", "second preliminary estimate", "final estimate / third closing",
          "preliminary benchmark estimate", "final benchmark"),
  Driver = c("—", "late survey responses", "late survey responses",
             "QCEW, March anchor only", "QCEW + rescaling + birth-death")
) |> kable(col.names = c("What you'll hear", "What BLS calls it", "What drives the change"), align = "c")
```

"final estimate" is final in the sense that — the benchmark is still coming; and the number in the news is usually the *preliminary* benchmark, which itself gets revised.

## The monthly cycle

CES "revises its initial monthly estimates twice, in the immediately succeeding 2 months, to incorporate additional sample receipts from respondents in the survey."[^11]

[^11]: BLS, *CES Frequently Asked Questions*. <https://www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cesfaq.htm>

![](fig-release-schedule.png)

\[WRITE — July 2026 is the example: the initial print said the economy *lost* 23,000 jobs; one month of additional survey responses flipped it to a *gain* of 21,000. Same month, same method, opposite sign.\]

## The annual benchmark

The final benchmark is published with the January preliminary estimates in early February, and affects **21 months** of previously published data, anchored to **March of the previous year**.[^12]

[^12]: BLS, *CES Frequently Asked Questions*. <https://www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cesfaq.htm>

![](fig-benchmark-window.png)

\[WRITE — the split almost nobody mentions: the 12 months up to the March anchor get the QCEW treatment; the 9 months after it are only rescaled and re-modelled. BofA states the consequence plainly: April–December 2025 payrolls "will not be revised based on the QCEW until 2027."[^13] So a payroll number is roughly eighteen months old before anyone checks it against tax records.\]

[^13]: BofA Global Research, *US Economic Viewpoint — QCEW: 2025 FAQs*, 3 September 2025.

![](fig-successive-windows.png)

Also worth folding in: **five years of seasonally adjusted data are recalculated** with every benchmark.[^14] Months far outside the 21-month window change every February from seasonal factors alone.

[^14]: BLS, *Employment Situation Quick Guide*. <https://www.bls.gov/bls/empsitquickguide.htm>

## Decomposition of Revisions

The decomposition is arithmetic on two vintages of the same month:

$$\underbrace{\text{SA}_{\text{latest}} - \text{SA}_{\text{first}}}_{\text{total revision}}
\;=\;
\underbrace{\text{NSA}_{\text{latest}} - \text{NSA}_{\text{first}}}_{\text{data: late responses}\,+\,\text{benchmark}}
\;+\;
\underbrace{\;\text{residual}\;}_{\text{seasonal-factor re-estimation}}$$

The unadjusted series never touches a seasonal factor, so anything that moves it is incoming data. Whatever is left when you subtract that from the adjusted revision is the adjustment changing its mind.

![](fig-revision-decomposition.png)

- The **drift** is data. Mean total revision **−61k**; mean data component **−50k**, mean factor component **−11k**. Payrolls have been revised down persistently, and late reporting plus the benchmark explains most of it.
- The **noise** is not. Mean absolute factor component is **48k** against **71k** for data, and the factor component is the *larger* of the two in **20 of 47 months**.
- The two pull in **opposite directions in 26 of 47 months** (correlation −0.46). The published revision routinely understates how much actually moved underneath it.

# IV. What happened in 2024–2026

| Benchmark (to March) | Preliminary |       Final        |
|:--------------------:|:-----------:|:------------------:|
|      March 2024      |  −818,000   |      −598,000      |
|      March 2025      |  −911,000   |      −862,000      |
|      March 2026      |   −79,000   | *pending Feb 2027* |

\[WRITE — the arc. A −911k revision fed a year of "the data is fake" arguments. BLS changed the birth-death model effective January 2026. The next preliminary benchmark came in at −79,000 and was released on 28 August 2026 to near-silence.\]

![Nonfarm payroll growth by industry, seasonally adjusted, expressed as a within-row z-score against each industry's own ten-year distribution (2015–2025, excluding 2020). Blue is a weaker month than that industry's own norm; red is stronger. Source: author's calculations.](figure1.png)

\[WHY THIS SITS HERE — Part IV turns on whether the labour market actually weakened, and this is evidence for that independent of any benchmark revision. Read it left to right.\
\
Two cautions before you lean on it: the reference distribution runs to 2025, so it contains the 2021–22 hiring boom, which makes "normal" look strong; and a z-score says *unusual for that industry*, not *large*. Say what the panel shows and let the reader weigh it.\]

\[THE OPEN QUESTION YOU CAN TAKE A POSITION ON — is that the model fix working, or just a calmer labour market? Note the Cleveland Fed finds 1980–2025 benchmark revisions have a mean of −4.51k and a median of −2.00k, and that structural-break tests find *no break* — so 2024–25 was extreme but not a regime change.[^15]\]

[^15]: Federal Reserve Bank of Cleveland, *BLS Benchmark Revisions: Is This Time Different?*, 2026. <https://www.clevelandfed.org/publications/economic-commentary/2026/ec-202612-bls-benchmark-revisions-is-this-time-different>

## Questions worth further exploration

**Is the CES sample shrinking, and does it matter?** The August 2026 technical note puts the sample at 119,000 businesses and 622,000 worksites, against 121,000 and 631,000 a year earlier, which is a decline of roughly 1.5%. Over the same period the stated coverage moved from "about one-third" of nonfarm payroll jobs to "approximately 26 percent." A 1.5% reduction in the sample cannot produce a shift of that size, so either the denominator changed or BLS restated the basis. This is a separate question from @tbl-response because that one is about who answers, this one is about how many are asked. The worry is that if the sample really is contracting, the standard error on every monthly print is widening.

**Can the adjustment be reproduced?** The seasonal adjustment tool X-13ARIMA-SEATS is public. The Census Bureau distributes the binary and the `seasonal` package wraps it in R.

